{
  "title": "What sits at the top of the S&P 500",
  "subtitle": "The ten largest companies in the index, every five years since it began",
  "convention": "Year-end standing. 1957 is the index's first year (it was expanded to 500 names in March 1957). The final frame is mid-2026.",
  "methodology": {
    "claim": "The ordered list of names in each frame is the sourced claim. Sector tags follow from what each company primarily sold in that year, not from a modern GICS label applied backwards.",
    "weights": "Bar lengths are each name's estimated share of total index market value. Values carrying \"est\": true are reconstructions, not reported figures. Documented anchors: AT&T at roughly 6.5% of a $172B index in 1957; IBM and Exxon tied near 2.9% with GE at 2.3% in 1990; Nvidia 7.5%, Microsoft 6.7%, Apple 5.6%, Amazon 4.3%, Alphabet 4.0% in 2025.",
    "top10Share": "Documented where cited (about 19% in 1990, about 23% at year-end 2000, about 32.5% in late 2025, above 37% in 2026). Other years are the sum of the estimated weights and should be read as approximate.",
    "conflicts": "Sources disagree on 1990: one ordering puts Exxon, then GE, then IBM; a separate set of weights has IBM and Exxon tied near 2.9% with GE at 2.3%. The ordering is the better-attested claim, so the weights were fitted to it rather than the other way round.",
    "employees": "Headcount is the total employees of the single largest company that year, global, approximate, drawn from the company's own reporting for that era.",
    "atoms": "\"Makes a physical thing\" marks companies whose primary product is a good that is manufactured, grown, or pulled out of the ground. Retail and distribution are marked false: selling someone else's goods is not making them. Software, search, advertising, banking, insurance, and telephone service are false. Fabless chip designers are true, since the product shipped is silicon.",
    "caveat": "Rankings for 1957 through 1985 are drawn from published financial history rather than a single machine-readable series. Treat the ordering as solid and the exact figures as approximate."
  },
